# macOS UI

Full notes: `docs/macos-client-patterns.md`.

- SwiftUI views describe UI only. Genealogy, SQLite, and identity stay in Go (FFI later).
- Keep `body` cheap and free of I/O. Put validation and actions on a model; inject a `GenealogyStore` protocol with a `FakeStore` for tests and previews.
- Prefer SwiftUI; wrap AppKit (panels, menus, `NSColor`) in small helpers.
- If a control will be UI-tested, give it a stable `.accessibilityIdentifier`.
- User-facing copy goes through typed `L10n` + String Catalogs. Follow `.agents/skills/add-localized-string/SKILL.md`. Do not hard-code English in views/models.
- Put new screens under `macos/App/Features/<Feature>/` (view + model together). Reusable `PV*` chrome lives in `DesignSystem/` (see `macos/App/DesignSystem/README.md`). FFI/store types stay in `Platform/`. Do not add more files to a flat target root.
- UI layering — `DesignSystem/Components|Recipes|Snowflakes/<Name>/` (one folder per control; no category nesting): [`.cursor/rules/design-system-layers.mdc`](design-system-layers.mdc). When adding or forking chrome, follow [`.agents/skills/add-ui-component/SKILL.md`](../../.agents/skills/add-ui-component/SKILL.md). When auditing an existing type for compose/refactor PRs, follow [`.agents/skills/evaluate-ui-component/SKILL.md`](../../.agents/skills/evaluate-ui-component/SKILL.md).
- Restoreable workspace places (sidebar destinations, deep pages, master–detail selection) go through `WorkspaceLocation` + `WorkspaceNavigation.go(to:)` (environment). Follow `.agents/skills/add-workspace-location/SKILL.md`.
- Unit tests go in `macos/ProvenenciaTests/` (Swift Testing). Follow `.agents/skills/add-swift-test/SKILL.md`. Do not co-locate `*Tests.swift` under `App/`.
- Go wire vocabulary is spelled once in Swift, as a typed enum, and views and models use the typed cases. `api/ffi/swift_vocabulary_test.go` is the index: every such enum is a row there, and the test fails if it drifts from Go. Most live in `Platform/ConclusionVocabulary.swift` (field states, reconciler reasons, Promote reason codes, seeded Property keys and terms, date qualifiers); others sit beside their owner (`PropertyValueType`, `EvidencePrimaryKind`, `CatalogList`, `PVMarkKey`), and omnibar hit kinds are `Platform/CatalogSearchKind.swift`. A new one goes in `Platform/` and gets a row in the test. The limits Swift mirrors from Go (`ArtifactRegionGeometry.maxRegionPoints`, `ProjectFiles.objectsDir`, `ThumbnailDecodePolicy.maxDeclaredEdge`) are pinned the same way.
- Read a catalog `relPath` only through `ProjectFiles.objectURL` (or `ThumbnailCache` / `CachedThumbnail`, which call it); it also refuses symlinks, Finder aliases and an execute bit. Never join it to `projectDir` yourself. Open an object in its own app only through `ProjectFiles.openObject`, which also refuses a name with no extension and an executable type (`IngestMediaPolicy.isExecutableType`) and reports why. Open a metadata link only through `CatalogMetadataEntry.openHref`, the web link Go computes; never build a URL from `valueText`.
- A Swift mirror of a Go rule (`TextMatch`, `FieldSlug`, `ProjectSlug`) keeps a fixture table in its enum (`TextMatch.fixtures`, `FieldSlug.fixtures`, `ProjectSlug.folderNameFixtures`); Go reads them in `api/ffi/swift_vocabulary_test.go` (`swiftFixturePairs`) when the mirror is wired there.
